n us in  the kingdom I imagine that if any one of  us including me were to kneel at Jesus's  feet as this man does and ask him what  do I need to live god's way he would  commend us on what we've already done he  would look at us deeply and he would  love us and then he would say he lacked  just one thing and Jesus would ask us I  think to give up that one thing that  impedes our faith that one thing that we  hold to so tightly that it defines us my  intelligence my work ethic my  accomplishments my service to others my  faith my family my athletic skill my  voice my wealth the list can go on in  her evening prayer reflection this past  Wednesday shall see to told the story I  will adapt to a different theme she  wrote a beautiful passage about our body  as what defines us she wrote a few years  ago I was in the hospital about to go  into surgery and I was lying there in  bed being wheeled away from my family  towards the anesthesia board and I felt  that my identity was falling away from  me I none of the possessions and  accessories that make me feel so safe my  clothes my jewelry my watch my purse I  had no friends or friends family around  me to affirm that I was a person who  loved  and mattered I didn't even have a book  or an iPod to distract me from the fact  that I was about to go through a surgery  all I had was a hospital gown a plastic  ID bracelet and an IV in my arm none of  which belonged to me and none of which  brought me any comfort shall story  captures so well the delicate nature of  our identities as we construct them  they're like castles built of feathers I  think I know who I am but my identity is  actually a random assemblage of every  compliment I've ever received every  behavior that was positively reinforced  every possession I had that others  admired every myth I've made up about  myself to avoid humiliation Jesus  however looks at us and with ultimate  love judges us judges what we're holding  on to he sees through us like glass he's  